What this process showed was that the solution to America's problems regarding sluggish projects is not more state or less state, more regulation or less regulation, but better state and better regulation.
It needs to be more difficult to use the court system to strangle development and the speed of construction approvals must increase.
At the same time having deep community input is essential to making sure the projercts that go ahead do so in the way that is least destructive to the lives and wellbeing of the people affected.
This is a topic where thinking in the left-right binary that is so relied upon in the west is unhelpful.
Energy abundance, alleviating poverty and building world class public transport are not ideological goals and we do ourselves a disservice in treating them as such.
To build a can-do, innovative, public welfare inclusive, green social market economy we need to put to rest the tendency for more and more bureaucratic quangos and start thinking about rewarding the job creators and the laborers.
The Singapore/China model of development at all cost is destructive for social cohesion and long term welfare and so is the California and Germany red tape fest.
Things which can be considered are closer public-private partnerships for designated national champions to build in the public interest and expedited lawsuit free automatic approval for citical green energy, transport and battery projects.
We know this can be done, Denmark and the Netherlands are rapidly transitioning towards net zero carbon emissions through a combination of public transit, solar, wind hydropower and nuclear, all without sacrificing the quality of life of ordinary citizens. 
There is no reason to think that in the richest country on earth we could not do the same.
Progressives and environmentalists need to get out of their own way and fully back the regulatory reforms that will greenlight the sustainable economy of the future in line with the Paris Climate Accord goals.
